#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176867} :activity/id #uuid "b5e5427c-d46e-5198-b0d9-c69a155fe586" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049991 :topic/id #uuid "245d7b8c-ac58-53e1-bbb6-af3096067f47" :topic/math-academy-id 3567} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177070 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of the cosecant function again.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nThe graph of the cosecant function has infinitely many asymptotes, and there is an infinite number of points where the function takes values of $-1$ and $1.$\n\nLet's write general expressions for these properties, by assuming $n$ represents an integer.\n\n**The Asymptotes of the Function**\n\nThe function has asymptotes when $x=0,\\pm \\pi, \\pm 2\\pi,\\ldots,$ etc. Therefore, a general equation for the asymptotes of the graph is\n\n$$\nx = n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $1$ when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period of $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = 1$ is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Points Where the Function Takes a Value of $\\mathbf{-1}$**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function takes a value of $-1$ when $x=-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ Since the function has a period $2\\pi,$ a general expression for the values of $x$ for which $\\csc x = -1$ is\n\n$$\nx = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} +2n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5e01b49e-464d-573e-a389-92bab4f6161c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6902 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8485b132-6a9a-5840-b8d5-354005537165" :step/math-academy-id 41806 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176868}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176053 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175699 :question/id #uuid "12dc1c50-69bc-54d8-8d60-a7fc144c9644" :question/math-academy-id "e-11008"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "92d6129b-a5c5-597a-86ee-3f19af20f326" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050172}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176487} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238055} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238056} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335825} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399398}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing the General Properties of the Cosecant Function"} :step/id #uuid "3c8301c9-9d8d-5faf-85bd-9953f54432dc" :step/math-academy-id 41807 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176869}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177071 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the graph of the function $y=\\csc x$ once more.\n\n![](images/67/67463d29b77a6db10538bcf5956f9bb8a40a5d41a2b7741c6de52bd336bcc550.png)\n\nAs previously mentioned, the cosecant function inherits its periodicity from the sine function, so the period is $2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/ce/ce59afb77eb33880d7e92d1c294b872ec3af898c43a2af6816ef63fee64abd3b.png)\n\nThe periodicity of $y=\\csc x$ means that $\\csc (x+2n\\pi ) = \\csc x$ for any number $x$ in the domain of $\\csc x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a00a960f-3462-59dc-9e77-5d7bf69c65bf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6903 :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/id #uuid "6312d083-7bf0-5033-ab8b-23dffd2c8040" :step/math-academy-id 41810 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176870}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176870 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176054 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175700 :question/id #uuid "a1713375-241f-5a53-a109-3c3d0f917411" :question/math-academy-id "e-11009"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "37434c3e-2269-5b08-94c5-7516df7879b2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176554} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238057} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335891} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335892}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/id #uuid "02d19625-6a8c-527d-95de-e106d7abf898" :step/math-academy-id 41811 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176899}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176878 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176060 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175706 :question/id #uuid "12eb5678-b62b-50ad-97bf-88768c4bf365" :question/math-academy-id "e-11015"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48a710f9-fed1-5fdb-b4c0-fd3ae2d4d8a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176353} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275437} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275438} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275439} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275440}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/id #uuid "9cf094b8-771c-510b-a5f8-f59eca4b156e" :step/math-academy-id 41841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176899 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177086 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *odd* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosecant is an odd function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\csc(-x) = -\\csc(x).\n$$\n\nOdd functions always have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This is indeed true of the cosecant function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/cb/cb63f7c0930524e9f54a65aad6d74784c403a6255f723b879d76586ad8ea598b.png)\n\nWe can use the oddness property of cosecant in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0e094388-438b-588a-9c7a-00cb1afc6f15" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7902 :tutorial/title "The Oddness Property of the Cosecant Function"} :step/id #uuid "755ad03e-ee78-55e9-9249-6a54b86b8b45" :step/math-academy-id 45527 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176878}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Cosecant Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176117}]}
